  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 AM EDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nOur upper low that's brought moisture into the area continues to \nmove southeastward slowly today so that it resides in the \nMid-Atlantic and Southeastern States by this evening. Vorticity\ncontinues to dampen across the area today and with east winds, \na thin layer of cloudiness is expected to reside across the \narea. This is until an area of dry air advection pushes through \nthis evening. RAP time sections from last night which showed \nthis erosion of moisture below 850 mb last evening and hanged \nonto low stratus, but it certainly makes sense that this \nboundary coming through this evening would erode the thin layer\nof lingering stratus.\n\nFollowing that, another vigorous upper shortwave dives southward \nalong the eastern coast of Lk MI later Fri. However, enough dry air \nresides across the area that it should be a dry passage. \nAdditionally, with it being on the front edge of a surface high \npressure system edging into the area, gradient winds look to stay \nweak.\n\nDuring this late week period, low teens Celsius at 850 mb could\nbe tapped into to, with the best chance of this happening today\nsouth of US-30 and west of US-31, where the better low level \nlapse rates reside. Otherwise, Friday appears to have weaker \nlow level lapse rates allowing for sub-70F degree highs. Highs \nsurpassing 70F appear to edge into the area for the weekend as \nthe shortwave dampens over Lk MI and the vorticity from the \nstrong nor'easter across the Northeast backs westward. Lows are \nexpected to be securely in the 40s from Friday night through \nSunday night so it'll feel cool at night then.\n\nMore substantial warm advection finally works its way into the \narea between Tuesday night and Wednesday as a Pacific trough \ndeepens and works northeastward. There's some question on timing\nof arrival of the warmer air so highs between Tuesday and \nWednesday still have some uncertainty. Increasing confidence of \nhighs surpassing 70F area-wide on Wednesday and perhaps \nThursday as 20 to 30% chance for highs >79 degrees occur from \nWhite county to Jay county. Thunderstorms could be possible \nbetween Tuesday night and Thursday depending on the timing of \nthe system into the area.",
